== Chapter 18

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[THE AGENCY OF THE WICKED EMPLOYED BY GOD, WHO BENDS THEIR MINDS TO EXECUTE HIS JUDGMENTS, WHILE HE REMAINS FREE FROM EVERY TAINT.#footnote[See Calvin, adv. Libertinos, cap. 15. 16., and Augustin. de Ordine, Lib. 1. and 2., where he discusses admirably the question whether the order of divine Providence includes all good and evil. (Beveridge’s note.)]]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[This last chapter of the First Book consists of three parts: I. Since it was said above that God bends all the reprobate, and even Satan himself, to his will, three objections are raised. First, that this happens by the permission, not by the will, of God. To this objection there is a twofold reply: the first, that angels and men, good and bad, do nothing but what God has appointed; the second, that all movements are secretly directed to their end by the hidden inspiration of God, sec. 1, 2. II. A second objection is that there are two contrary wills in God, if by a secret counsel he decrees what he openly forbids by his law. This objection refuted, sec. 3. III. The third objection is that God is made the author of all wickedness, when he is said not only to use the agency of the wicked, but also to govern their counsels and feelings, and that therefore the wicked are unjustly punished. This objection refuted in the last section.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The carnal mind the source of the objections raised against the Providence of God. A first objection, which makes a distinction between the #emph[permission] and the #emph[will] of God, refuted. Angels and men, good and bad, do nothing but what God has decreed. This proved by examples.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. All hidden movements directed to their end by the unseen but righteous impulse of God. Examples, with answers to objections.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. These objections spring from a spirit of pride and blasphemy. The objection that there must be two contrary wills in God, refuted. Why the one simple will of God seems to us as if it were manifold.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. The objection that God is the author of sin, refuted by examples. Augustine’s answer and warning.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[From] other passages, in which God is said to draw or bend Satan himself, and all the reprobate, to his will, a more difficult question arises. For the carnal mind can scarcely grasp how God, when he acts through them, contracts no taint from their impurity; indeed, how, in a common work, he is free from all guilt, and can justly condemn his own agents. Hence a distinction has been invented between #emph[doing] and #emph[permitting], because to many it seemed altogether inexplicable how Satan and all the wicked are so under the hand and authority of God that he directs their malice to whatever end he pleases, and uses their crimes to carry out his judgments. The modesty of those who are alarmed in this way at the appearance of absurdity might perhaps be excused, if they did not try to clear the justice of God of every shadow of reproach by defending an untruth. It seems absurd that man should be blinded by the will and command of God, and yet be punished at once for his blindness. So they take refuge in the evasion that this is done only by the permission, and not also by the will, of God. But God himself, openly declaring that he #emph[does] this, rejects the evasion. That men do nothing except at the secret nod of God, and do not discuss and deliberate on anything but what he has already decreed within himself and brings to pass by his secret direction, is proved by countless clear passages of Scripture. What we quoted earlier from the Psalms, that he does whatever pleases him, certainly extends to all the actions of men. If God is the arbiter of peace and war, as is said there, and that without any exception, who will dare to say that men are carried along at random by a blind impulse, while He is unaware or inactive? But the matter will be made clearer by particular examples. From the first chapter of Job we learn that Satan presents himself before God to receive his orders, just as the angels do, who obey of their own accord. The manner and the end are different, but still the fact is that he cannot attempt anything without the will of God. But though afterwards his power to afflict the saint seems to be only a bare permission, yet since the saying is true, “The #smallcaps[Lord] gave, and the #smallcaps[Lord] has taken away; as it pleased the #smallcaps[Lord], so it has been done” (Job 1:21), we infer that God was the author of that trial, of which Satan and wicked robbers were merely the instruments. Satan’s aim is to drive the saint to madness through despair. The Sabeans cruelly and wickedly make a sudden raid to rob another man of his goods. Job acknowledges that he was stripped by God of all his property, and brought to poverty, because such was God’s pleasure. Therefore, whatever men or Satan himself may plot, God holds the helm, and makes all their efforts serve the carrying out of his judgments. God wills that the treacherous Ahab should be deceived; the devil offers his services for that purpose, and is sent with a definite command to be a lying spirit in the mouth of all the prophets (1 Kgs 22:20). If the blinding and madness of Ahab is a judgment from God, the fiction of bare permission is at an end; for it would be ridiculous for a judge only to permit, and not also to decree, what he wishes to be done at the very time that he commits its execution to his officers. The Jews intended to destroy Christ. Pilate and the soldiers gave in to their fury; yet the disciples confess in solemn prayer that all the wicked did nothing but what the hand and counsel of God had decreed (Acts 4:28), just as Peter had said before in his sermon, that Christ was delivered to death by the determined counsel and foreknowledge of God (Acts 2:23); in other words, that God, to whom all things are known from the beginning, had knowingly and willingly determined what the Jews carried out. He repeats the same thing elsewhere: “In this way God has fulfilled what He foretold through all the prophets, saying that His Christ would suffer” (Acts 3:18). Absalom, incestuously defiling his father’s bed, commits a detestable crime (2 Sam 16:22). God, however, declares that it was his work; for the words are, “You have acted in secret, but I will do this thing in broad daylight before all Israel.”#footnote[2 Sam 12:12; Jer 50:25; Isa 5:26; 10:5; 19:25; 2 Sam 16:10; 1 Kgs 11:31; 1 Sam 2:34.] The cruelties of the Chaldeans in Judea are declared by Jeremiah to be the work of God. For this reason Nebuchadnezzar is called the servant of God. God often cries out that by his whistle, by the blast of his trumpet, by his authority and command, the wicked are stirred up to war. He calls the Assyrian the rod of his anger, and the axe that he wields in his hand. The overthrow of the city and the fall of the temple he calls his own work. David, not murmuring against God, but acknowledging him to be a just judge, confesses that the curses of Shimei are uttered by his orders. “The Lord,” he says, “has ordered him to curse.” Often in sacred history whatever happens is said to come from the Lord, as the revolt of the ten tribes, the death of Eli’s sons, and very many others of the same kind. Those who have a fair acquaintance with the Scriptures see that, for the sake of brevity, I am bringing forward only a few passages out of many; and from these it is perfectly clear that it is the merest trifling to substitute a bare permission for the providence of God, as if he sat in a watchtower waiting for chance events, while his judgments depended on the will of man.

2\. With regard to secret movements: what Solomon says of the heart of a king (Prov 21:1), that it is turned this way and that as God sees fit, certainly applies to the whole human race, and has the same force as if he had said that God, by his secret inspiration, directs to his own end whatever we conceive in our minds. And certainly, if he did not work inwardly in the minds of men, it could not rightly have been said that he takes away speech from the truthful, and prudence from the aged—takes away the heart from the princes of the earth, so that they wander in trackless ways (Isa 29:14). To the same effect, we often read that men are afraid when He fills their hearts with terror (Ps 107:40; Ezek 7:26; Lev 26:36). So David left the camp of Saul without anyone knowing it, because a sleep from God had fallen on them all (1 Sam 26:12). But nothing could be clearer than the many passages which declare that he blinds the minds of men, strikes them with dizziness, makes them drunk with a spirit of stupor, drives them mad, and hardens their hearts (Rom 1:26; 11:8). Even these expressions many would limit to permission, as if, by deserting the reprobate, he allowed them to be blinded by Satan. But since the Holy Spirit expressly says that the blindness and madness are inflicted by the just judgment of God, that solution is altogether inadmissible. He is said to have hardened the heart of Pharaoh, to have hardened it still more, and to have made it firm. Some evade these expressions with a foolish quibble: because Pharaoh is elsewhere said to have hardened his own heart (Exod 8:15), they make his will the cause of the hardening; as if the two things did not agree perfectly with each other, though in different senses—namely, that man, though acted on by God, at the same time also acts. But I turn the objection back on those who make it. If to harden means only bare permission, the stubbornness itself will not properly belong to Pharaoh. Now, could anything be more feeble and empty than to interpret it as if Pharaoh had only allowed himself to be hardened? We may add that Scripture cuts off every pretext for such quibbles: “I,” says the Lord, “will harden his heart” (Exod 4:21). So also Moses says of the inhabitants of the land of Canaan that they went out to battle because the Lord had hardened their hearts (Josh 11:20). The same thing is repeated by another prophet: “He turned their hearts to hate His people” (Ps 105:25). In the same way, in Isaiah, he says of the Assyrian, “I will send him against a godless nation; against a people destined for My rage I will give him orders to take spoils and seize plunder” (Isa 10:6); not that he means to teach wicked and obstinate men to obey willingly, but because he bends them to carry out his judgments, just as if they carried their orders engraved on their minds. And so it appears that they are impelled by the sure appointment of God. I admit, indeed, that God often acts in the reprobate through the agency of Satan; but in such a way that Satan himself performs his part just as he is impelled by God, and succeeds only so far as is given him. The evil spirit that troubled Saul is said to be from the Lord (1 Sam 16:14), to show that Saul’s madness was a just punishment from God. Satan is also said to blind the minds of those who do not believe (2 Cor 4:4). But how so, unless because the efficacy of error is sent from God himself, making those who refuse to obey the truth believe a lie? In the first sense it is said, “But if the prophet is deceived and speaks a message, it was I, the #smallcaps[Lord], who deceived that prophet” (Ezek 14:9). In the second sense he is said to have given men over to a reprobate mind, and cast them into vile lusts (Rom 1:28), because he is the principal author of his own just vengeance, whereas Satan is only his agent.#footnote[See Calvin on Ps 141:4. (Beveridge’s note.)] But since in the Second Book,#footnote[Chapter 4 sec. 3, 4. (Beveridge’s note.)] when we discuss the question of the freedom or bondage of man’s will, this subject will be considered again, the little that has now been said seems to be all that the occasion requires. The sum of the whole is this: since the will of God is said to be the cause of all things, all the counsels and actions of men must be held to be governed by his providence; so that he not only exerts his power in the elect, who are guided by the Holy Spirit, but also forces the reprobate to do him service.

3\. As I have so far stated only what is plainly and unambiguously taught in Scripture, those who do not hesitate to brand what the sacred oracles teach in this way had better beware what kind of verdict they presume to pass. If, under a pretense of ignorance, they seek the praise of modesty, what greater arrogance can be imagined than to utter one word against the authority of God—to say, for instance, “I think otherwise,” or “I would rather this subject were not touched”? But if they openly blaspheme, what will they gain by spitting at heaven? Such insolence, indeed, is not new. In all ages there have been wicked and profane men who have rabidly attacked this part of doctrine. But what the Spirit declared long ago by the mouth of David (Ps 51:6) they will find by experience to be true—God will prevail when he is judged. David indirectly rebukes the madness of those whose license is so unbridled that from their groveling spot of earth they not only plead against God, but claim for themselves the right to condemn him. At the same time, he briefly indicates that the blasphemies they belch out against heaven do not reach God, but that he disperses the mists of their slanders, and makes his justice shine forth. Even our faith, because it is founded on the sacred word of God, is superior to the whole world (1 John 5:4), and can look down from its height on such mists.

Their first objection—that if nothing happens without the will of God, he must have two contrary wills, decreeing by a secret counsel what he has openly forbidden in his law—is easily disposed of. But before I reply to it, I would again remind my readers that this quibble is aimed not at me, but at the Holy Spirit, who certainly dictated this confession to that holy man Job, “As it pleased the #smallcaps[Lord], so it has been done” (Job 1:21), when, after being plundered by robbers, he acknowledges that their injustice and wrongdoing were a just chastisement from God. And what does Scripture say elsewhere? The sons of Eli “would not listen to their father, since the #smallcaps[Lord] intended to put them to death” (1 Sam 2:25). Another prophet also cries out, “Our God is in heaven; He does as He pleases” (Ps 115:3). I have already shown clearly enough that God is the author of all those things which, according to these objectors, happen only by his inactive permission. He testifies that he creates light and darkness, forms good and evil (Isa 45:7); that no evil happens which he has not done (Amos 3:6). Let them tell me whether God exercises his judgments willingly or unwillingly. As Moses teaches that he who is accidentally killed by the blow of an axe is delivered by God into the hand of the one who strikes him (Deut 19:5), so the whole church, as Luke relates, declares that Herod and Pontius Pilate conspired “to carry out what Your hand and counsel had decided beforehand would happen” (Acts 4:28). And, in truth, if Christ was not crucified by the will of God, where is our redemption? Yet the will of God is not at odds with itself. It undergoes no change. He does not pretend not to will what he wills; but while in himself the will is one and undivided, to us it appears manifold, because, from the weakness of our intellect, we cannot grasp how, though in a different way, he wills and does not will the very same thing. Paul calls the calling of the Gentiles a hidden mystery, and shortly after adds that in it the manifold wisdom of God was made known (Eph 3:10). Since, because of the dullness of our understanding, the wisdom of God seems manifold (or, as an old translator rendered it, multiform), are we therefore to dream of some variation in God, as if he either changed his counsel or disagreed with himself? No; when we cannot grasp how God can will that to be done which he forbids us to do, let us call to mind our weakness, and remember that the light in which he dwells is not without cause called inaccessible (1 Tim 6:16), because it is shrouded in darkness. Hence all pious and modest men will readily accept the view of Augustine: “Man sometimes with a good will wishes something that God does not will, as when a good son wishes his father to live, while God wills him to die. Again, it may happen that man with a bad will wishes what God wills righteously, as when a bad son wishes his father to die, and God also wills it. The former wishes what God does not will; the latter wishes what God also wills. And yet the dutiful love of the former is more in keeping with the good will of God, though he wills something different, than the unnatural desire of the latter, though he wills the same thing; so much do approval and condemnation depend on what it is fitting for man to will, and what for God, and to what end the will of each is directed. For the things which God rightly wills, he accomplishes through the evil wills of bad men” (#emph[August. Enchirid. ad Laurent. cap.] 101). He had said a little before (cap. 100) that the apostate angels by their revolt, and all the reprobate, as far as they themselves were concerned, did what God did not will; but that, with regard to his omnipotence, it was impossible for them to do so: for while they act against the will of God, his will is accomplished in them. Hence he exclaims, “Great is the work of God, exquisite in all he wills (Ps 111:2)! So, in a wonderful and inexpressible way, what is done against his will is not done without his will, because it could not be done if he did not permit it; nor does he permit it unwillingly, but willingly; nor would He who is good permit evil to be done, were he not omnipotent to bring good out of evil” (#emph[August. Enchirid. ad Laurent. cap.] 100).

4\. In the same way another objection is solved, or rather vanishes of its own accord—namely: if God not only uses the agency of the wicked, but also governs their counsels and feelings, he is the author of all their sins; and therefore men, in carrying out what God has decreed, are unjustly condemned, because they are obeying his will. Here #emph[will] is wrongly confused with #emph[precept], though it is obvious from countless examples that there is the greatest difference between them.#footnote[The French is, “Car ils meslent perversement le commandement de Dieu avec son vouloir secret, veu qu’il appert par exemples infinis qu’il y a bien longue distance et diversité de l’un à l’autre;” for they perversely confuse the command of God with his secret will, though it is clear from countless examples that there is a great distance and difference between them. (Beveridge’s note.)] When Absalom defiled his father’s bed (2 Sam 16:22), though God was pleased in this way to avenge the adultery of David, he did not therefore command a depraved son to commit incest, unless perhaps with respect to David, as David himself says of Shimei’s curses. For while he confesses that Shimei acts by the order of God (2 Sam 16:10), he by no means commends the obedience, as if that insolent dog had been obeying a divine command; but, recognizing in his tongue the scourge of God, he submits patiently to be chastised. Thus we must hold that while God, by means of the wicked, performs what he had secretly decreed, they are not excusable, as if they were obeying his precept, which they deliberately violate according to their lust.

How these things which men do perversely are of God, and are ruled by his secret providence, is strikingly shown in the election of King Jeroboam (1 Kgs 12:20), in which the rashness and madness of the people are severely condemned for perverting the order sanctioned by God, and treacherously revolting from the family of David. And yet we know it was God’s will that Jeroboam should be anointed. Hence the apparent contradiction in the words of Hosea (Hos 8:4; 13:11): while God complained that that kingdom was set up without his knowledge, and against his will, he elsewhere declares that he had given King Jeroboam in his anger. How shall we reconcile the two things—that Jeroboam’s reign was not of God, and yet God appointed him king? In this way: the people could not revolt from the family of David without shaking off a yoke divinely laid on them, and yet God himself was not deprived of the power to punish the ingratitude of Solomon in this way. We see, therefore, how God, while not willing treachery, with another end in view justly wills the revolt; and so Jeroboam, by unexpectedly receiving the sacred anointing, is urged to aspire to the kingdom. For this reason the sacred history says that God stirred up an enemy to deprive the son of Solomon of part of the kingdom (1 Kgs 11:23). Let the reader carefully weigh both points: how, as it was the will of God that the people should be ruled by the hand of one king, their being torn into two parties was contrary to his will; and yet how this same will began the revolt. For certainly, when Jeroboam, who had no such thought, is urged by the prophet, by word and by the oil of anointing, to hope for the kingdom, this was not done without the knowledge or against the will of God, who had expressly commanded it; and yet the rebellion of the people is justly condemned, because they revolted from the descendants of David as if against the will of God. For this reason it is added afterwards that when Rehoboam haughtily spurned the requests of the people, “this turn of events was from the #smallcaps[Lord], to fulfill the word He had spoken through Ahijah” (1 Kgs 12:15). See how sacred unity was violated against the will of God, while at the same time, with his will, the ten tribes were alienated from the son of Solomon. To this might be added another similar example—namely, the murder of the sons of Ahab, and the extermination of his whole line, with the consent, or rather the active hands, of the people (2 Kgs 10:7). Jehu says truly, “Not a word the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken against the house of Ahab will fail, for the #smallcaps[Lord] has done what He promised through His servant Elijah” (2 Kgs 10:10). And yet, with good reason, he rebukes the citizens of Samaria for having lent their help: “You#super[+] are innocent. It was I who conspired against my master and killed him. But who killed all these?”

If I am not mistaken, I have already shown clearly how the same act at once exposes the guilt of man and displays the righteousness of God. Modest minds will always be satisfied with Augustine’s answer: “Since the Father delivered up the Son, Christ his own body, and Judas his Master, why in such a case is God just and man guilty, if not because in the one act which they did, the reasons for which they did it are different?” (#emph[August. Ep. 48, ad Vincentium]). If any are more perplexed by what we now say—namely, that there is no consent between God and man when by His righteous impulse man does what he ought not to do—let them heed what Augustine observes elsewhere: “Who can keep from trembling at those judgments, when God does as he pleases even in the hearts of the wicked, while at the same time rendering to them according to their deeds?” (#emph[De Grat. et lib. Arbit. ad Valent.] c. 20). And certainly, in the treachery of Judas, there is just as little ground to throw the blame for the crime on God, because He was both pleased that his Son should be delivered up to death, and did deliver him, as to give Judas the credit for our redemption. Hence Augustine elsewhere truly observes that in this examination God looks not to what men could do, or to what they did, but to what they wished to do, so taking account of their will and purpose. Those to whom this seems harsh had better consider how far their peevishness deserves to be tolerated when, because it exceeds their capacity, they reject a matter clearly taught by Scripture, and object to truths being set forth which God would never have ordered his prophets and apostles to teach if they were not useful to know. Our true wisdom is to embrace with meek teachableness, and without reservation, whatever the Holy Scriptures have handed down. Those who give free rein to their insolence, an insolence plainly directed against God, do not deserve a longer refutation.

#emph[END OF THE FIRST BOOK.]
